TI  - Enhancing nitrogen removal in low C/N wastewater via carbon resource recovery from biochar-mediated anaerobic digestion of discarded cefradine residues.
AU  - Shi, Yongsen
AU  - Pang, Boyuan
AU  - Jia, Yanyan
AU  - Zheng, Zhinan
AU  - Quan, Haoting
AU  - Qi, Peng
AU  - Hao, Tianwei
AU  - Sun, Lianpeng
AU  - Lu, Hui
JO  - Water research
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.1016/j.watres.2025.124641
AB  - Carbon source limitation remains a key bottleneck for efficient nitrogen removal in low C/N wastewater treatment. Discarded medicines, rich in excipients, represent a potential carbon source, but their recovery is challenged by residual pharmaceutical toxicity. In this study, a novel integrated strategy was proposed that combines carbon recovery from discarded cefradine medicine residues (DCMR) with nitrogen removal in low C/N municipal wastewater (LCNMW). Thermo-alkaline pretreatment effectively degraded cefradine and reduced biotoxicity, producing sucrose-rich DCMR, and then DCMR were subjected to biochar-mediated anaerobic digestion. Biochar alleviated acid inhibition and maintained stable anaerobic digestion performance, enabling the production of methane (286.8 mL/g COD) and bioavailable carbon-rich fermentation liquid (4081.3 mg/L COD). The biochar's porous structure and conductivity not only facilitated the enrichment of acidogenic bacteria (e.g., Mesotoga), antibiotic-degrading and electroactive microbes (e.g., Desulfovibrio, Syntrophobacter), and methanogens (e.g., Methanobacterium, Methanosaeta), but also stimulated microbial interspecies hydrogen transfer and direct interspecies electron transfer. These accelerated volatile fatty acids consumption, cefradine removal, and methane production. Subsequently, fermentation liquid was used as the supplementary carbon source for LCNMW biotreatment, improving total nitrogen (TN) removal by over 30%, and effluent concentrations of COD, TN, and NH₄⁺-N met local discharge standards. Enhanced TN removal was supported by the increased abundances of nitrification and denitrification genes (e.g., hao, nxr, nar, nir). The low ecological risks and non-biotoxicity of final effluent, obvious costs saving of commercial carbon source, as well as great potential of carbon emissions reduction confirmed the environmental and economic sustainability of whole process. This study provides a promising approach for coupling hazardous waste valorization with enhanced nitrogen removal in low C/N wastewater treatment systems.
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.watres.2025.124641
